\subsection*{System Overview}
The CKKS GPU Homomorphic Evolution Engine provides fully encrypted computation for
digital organism evolution. All organism states, traits, genomes, and historical
records remain encrypted throughout execution. The engine integrates RNS-CKKS
polynomial arithmetic, CUDA-accelerated NTT transforms, diagonal matrix encodings,
and homomorphic nonlinear approximations to support large-scale encrypted evolution.

\subsection*{1. SIMD Slot Geometry and Ciphertext Structure}
CKKS ciphertexts use a cyclotomic ring of dimension:
\[
N = 2^{15} = 32768,
\]
yielding:
\[
N/2 = 2^{14} = 16384 \text{ complex SIMD slots}.
\]

Each ciphertext encodes:
\[
\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t),\quad
\tilde{\mathbf{g}}_i(t),\quad
\tilde{\mathbf{H}}_i(t),
\]
with all components encrypted under the RNS-CKKS variant (HPS or Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song).

\subsection*{2. GPU Memory Layout and RNS Integration}
Residue Number System (RNS) decomposition eliminates big-integer arithmetic by
representing ciphertext coefficients as vectors of 64-bit residues:
\[
\mathbf{c} = (c^{(0)}, c^{(1)}, \ldots, c^{(L)}),
\]
where each $c^{(\ell)}$ resides in a distinct modulus $q_\ell$.

GPU memory layout includes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item contiguous RNS coefficient blocks,
    \item precomputed NTT twiddle factors,
    \item Galois rotation keys for diagonal matrix evaluation,
    \item bootstrapping keys and modulus-raising keys.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{3. CUDA/NTT Polynomial Evaluation Pipeline}
The evolution kernel \texttt{CKKS\_Evolve\_Engine} executes polynomial operations
entirely in the NTT domain:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Forward NTT:
    \[
    \mathbf{a} \rightarrow \mathrm{NTT}(\mathbf{a})
    \]
    \item Pointwise multiplication:
    \[
    \mathbf{c} = \mathbf{a} \odot \mathbf{b}
    \]
    \item Inverse NTT only when rescaling is required:
    \[
    \mathbf{c} \rightarrow \mathrm{INTT}(\mathbf{c})
    \]
    \item RNS rescaling:
    \[
    \tilde{c} \rightarrow \mathrm{Rescale}(\tilde{c})
    \]
\end{enumerate}

This minimizes transform cycles and maximizes GPU coalescing efficiency.

\subsection*{4. Homomorphic Matrix-Vector Evolution}
Encrypted interaction matrices $A,B,C$ are encoded using the Halevi–Shoup diagonal
method. Each diagonal is stored as a rotated vector, enabling:
\[
A\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t) =
\sum_{d=0}^{D-1}
\mathrm{Rot}(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t), d)
\cdot \tilde{A}_d,
\]
where $\mathrm{Rot}(\cdot)$ uses Galois evaluation keys.

Evolution update:
\[
\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t+1) =
\mathrm{Eval}\big(
A\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t) +
B\tilde{\mathbf{g}}_i(t) +
C\tilde{\mathcal{E}}(t)
\big).
\]

\subsection*{5. Homomorphic Nonlinear Operator $\sigma(\cdot)$}
Nonlinear mutation dynamics use Chebyshev polynomial approximations:
\[
\sigma(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t)) \approx
\sum_{k=0}^{K} a_k T_k(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t)),
\]
where $T_k$ are Chebyshev polynomials evaluated via Paterson–Stockmeyer scheduling
to minimize multiplicative depth.

Mutation update:
\[
\tilde{\mathbf{g}}_i(t+1) =
\tilde{\mathbf{g}}_i(t) +
\mathrm{Eval}\big(\sigma(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_i(t))\big).
\]

\subsection*{6. Noise Budget Decay and Bootstrapping Threshold}
Noise budget evolves as:
\[
\mathcal{B}(t+1) = \mathcal{B}(t) - \Delta_{\text{op}}.
\]

Bootstrapping is triggered when:
\[
\ell \le \ell_{\min} = 2,
\]
ensuring sufficient headroom for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item slot-to-coefficient transforms,
    \item encrypted sine/cosine evaluation,
    \item polynomial refresh operations.
\end{itemize}

GPU bootstrapping latency:
\[
2.48\text{ ms per ciphertext},
\]
consistent with optimized NTT kernels on high-end accelerators.

